---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import BillSplitCalculator from '@/components/tools/everyday/BillSplitCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="bill-split-calculator">
  <BillSplitCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to split a bill by item</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Switch to the <strong>Split by item</strong> tab.</li>
      <li>
        Add everyone at the table. Names are only labels and stay in your browser, so first names or
        nicknames are fine.
      </li>
      <li>
        Enter each line from the check with its price. You can group items, for example all the
        drinks, if the bill shows them that way.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tap the names of the people who shared each item. A dish tapped for three people is divided
        three ways. The <strong>Everyone</strong> button assigns a shared platter in one tap.
      </li>
      <li>
        Set the tax and tip. Tax can be a percentage or the exact amount on the bill, and the tip can
        be calculated before or after tax.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Each person gets a card with their items, their share of the tax and tip, and the total they
      owe. Copy the whole split to paste into a group chat.
    </p>

    <h2>Why tax and tip are shared proportionally</h2>
    <p>
      If the food is split by item but the tax and tip evenly, the person who ate least pays too
      much. This bill split calculator gives each person the same fraction of the tax and tip as
      their fraction of the subtotal:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>person total = own items + tax × (own items ÷ subtotal) + tip × (own items ÷ subtotal)</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      If your food is 30% of the subtotal, you pay 30% of the tax and 30% of the tip. Nobody can gain
      by ordering differently, and the parts always add up to the exact bill.
    </p>

    <h3>What happens to the odd cents</h3>
    <p>
      Proportional shares are almost never whole cents. The calculator computes exact shares, rounds
      each one down to the cent, then gives the remaining cents to the people with the largest
      fractional parts. This is the largest-remainder method used in apportionment. Nobody pays more
      than one extra cent, and the personal totals add up to the exact bill, which matters when one
      person pays by card.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Item</th>
            <th scope="col">Price</th>
            <th scope="col">Shared by</th>
            <th scope="col">Alex</th>
            <th scope="col">Bailey</th>
            <th scope="col">Casey</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Margherita pizza</td>
            <td>$18.00</td>
            <td>Alex</td>
            <td>$18.00</td>
            <td>$0</td>
            <td>$0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Sharing platter</td>
            <td>$24.50</td>
            <td>All three</td>
            <td>$8.17</td>
            <td>$8.17</td>
            <td>$8.16</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pasta</td>
            <td>$16.00</td>
            <td>Bailey</td>
            <td>$0</td>
            <td>$16.00</td>
            <td>$0</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Two cocktails</td>
            <td>$21.00</td>
            <td>Casey</td>
            <td>$0</td>
            <td>$0</td>
            <td>$21.00</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Subtotal</strong></td>
            <td><strong>$79.50</strong></td>
            <td></td>
            <td><strong>$26.17</strong></td>
            <td><strong>$24.17</strong></td>
            <td><strong>$29.16</strong></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      With 8.25% tax ($6.56) and an 18% pre-tax tip ($14.31), the $100.37 total splits to about $33.04
      for Alex, $30.51 for Bailey and $36.82 for Casey. An even split of $33.46 each would move about
      $2.50 from the cheapest order to the most expensive one.
    </p>

    <h2>When an even split is the right answer</h2>
    <p>
      Itemising is not always worth the effort. If everyone ate about the same, or precision would be
      rude in the group, use the <strong>Split evenly</strong> tab: a subtotal, a tax rate, a tip and a
      headcount. It also allocates to the exact cent, so the shares add up.
    </p>

    <h2>Common questions the numbers answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Someone only had a drink: assign the drink to that person alone, and their tax and tip shares
        get smaller automatically.
      </li>
      <li>
        An item is unassigned: the calculator warns you, names the item and keeps it out of all
        totals, so you can see the gap between the bill and what the group pays.
      </li>
      <li>
        The bill has a service charge: enter it as a fixed tax amount and set the tip to 0%, or add a
        tip on top.
      </li>
      <li>
        One person pays and gets paid back: copy the split. Each line is a name and an amount, ready
        to paste into a payment app request.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Names, prices and assignments stay in this browser tab, and closing the page discards them.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
